-        <p>Nib fills, signs, and redacts PDFs entirely on this computer. Your
-          documents are never uploaded.</p>
-
-        <h4>What a Nib signature proves</h4>
-        <p>When you <strong>Finalize &amp; sign</strong>, Nib certifies the document so any
-          later change is detectable. A valid signature shows three things:</p>
-        <ul>
-          <li><strong>Tamper-evidence</strong> — the document hasn't changed since it was signed.</li>
-          <li><strong>Key-holder</strong> — it was signed by whoever holds your signing key.</li>
-          <li><strong>Time</strong> — when it was signed (independently verifiable if you add a timestamp authority).</li>
-        </ul>
-
-        <h4>What it doesn't prove</h4>
-        <p>Your signing identity is <strong>self-generated on this machine</strong> — it is
-          <strong>not</strong> vouched for by a certificate authority or any third party. So a
-          Nib signature does <strong>not</strong> establish your legal identity to someone who
-          doesn't already trust your key, and it is not a qualified electronic signature (QES).
-          It proves the document is intact and was signed by your key — not who you are to a
-          stranger.</p>
-
-        <h4>Co-signing with other people</h4>
-        <p>A document can be signed by <strong>two or more people</strong>. Each signature
-          names the one party that signer accepted, in a visible block on the page. Where two
-          parties sign together, each names the other; where more than two do, the parties are
-          the ones named by whoever convened the proceeding — so signers who never connected to
-          each other <strong>have not checked each other's identity</strong>, and no signature
-          claims they did.</p>
-        <p>The same trust limits apply to every signature on a co-signed document: each proves
-          its own signer held their key and that the document has not changed since, and none
-          of them establishes anyone's legal identity to a stranger.</p>
-
-        <h4>Working with several documents</h4>
-        <p>Opening a document <strong>adds</strong> it — the one you had stays open. A strip
-          of tabs appears above the page once there are two; click one to switch. Each
-          document keeps its own page, zoom, scroll position and typed values while you are
-          on another.</p>
-        <p><strong>Close view</strong> puts down the document you are looking at and moves
-          to the next tab; <strong>Close all</strong> puts down every one. With a single
-          document open there is just <strong>Close</strong>. Reloading this window brings
-          every open document back — they live in the Nib process on your computer, not in
-          the page. Nib holds eight documents, or 512 MB of them, whichever comes first.</p>
-
-        <p class="libhint">Nib is free software under the AGPLv3 and bundles third-party
-          open-source components.</p>
